Use Three.js to implement a 3D duck shooting gallery game. Load the Duck.glb model, generate multiple moving ducks, and allow the player to shoot by clicking.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/Duck.glb;
(2) After loading is complete, clone 5 duck model instances and distribute them throughout the scene;
(3) After loading is complete, set window.__3D_STATE__.modelLoaded = true;

[Scene Setup]
(4) Set the scene background to sky blue (0x87CEEB);
(5) Create a shooting gallery backdrop wall (BoxGeometry, width 20 x height 10 x depth 0.5, dark brown 0x8B4513), positioned at z=-5;
(6) Add a ground plane at the bottom (green 0x228B22);
(7) Fix the camera at position (0, 3, 8), facing the center of the scene; it must not be movable;
(8) Use DirectionalLight + AmbientLight for illumination;

[Duck Movement]
(9) The 5 ducks move from left to right along the X-axis (from x=-12 to x=12, cyclically resetting to the left side after reaching the boundary);
(10) Distribute the 5 ducks at different heights: y = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5;
(11) Each duck has a different speed: 2, 3, 4, 3.5, and 2.5 units/second, respectively;
(12) Scale the ducks to 0.8 and orient them to face the direction of movement (to the right);

[Shooting Interaction]
(13) Clicking the screen casts a ray (Raycaster) to detect whether any duck is hit;
(14) After a duck is hit:
  a) That duck plays a "falling" animation: it rotates downward and falls (using a tween or manual interpolation: rotation.z rotates by 90 degrees and y decreases to -2 within 1 second);
  b) score += 1;
  c) After 3 seconds, the duck reappears on the left side at its original height and resumes moving;
(15) When a click does not hit a duck, missCount += 1;

[Timer and Game Flow]
(16) The game has a 60-second countdown (id="timer"), updated every second;
(17) When the countdown ends, display a "Game Over" panel (id="gameOver") showing the final score and hit rate;
(18) The "Restart" button (id="restartBtn") resets all state;

[HUD Display]
(19) The HUD in the upper-left corner displays:
  - Score (id="score")
  - Miss count (id="missCount")
  - Countdown (id="timer")
  - Number of active ducks (id="activeDucks")

[Crosshair]
(20) Display a crosshair at the center of the screen (implemented with CSS); the crosshair follows the mouse when it moves (or remains fixed at the center);

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean (whether the model has finished loading)
  - score: number (score)
  - missCount: number (number of misses)
  - timeLeft: number (remaining time in seconds)
  - activeDucks: number (current number of active ducks, 0-5)
  - gameOver: boolean (whether the game is over)
  - duckPositions: Array<{x,y,z}> (current positions of the 5 ducks)
  - hitRate: number (hit rate from 0-1, score/(score+missCount), or 0 when no shots have been fired)

Output a single HTML file. Place this file in the same directory as the assets/ folder and run it through a local HTTP server.
